d) A proof of eligibility prescribed at Point a, Clause 1, Article 10 of this Law;

dd) A certificate of professional training in mediation or dialogue prescribed in Point d, Clause 1,
Article 10 of this Law.

3. Based on the need to appoint a mediator, the court which receives an application for appointment of mediator shall choose a qualified person and then request the Chief Judge of the Provincial People’s Court to consider appointment.

4. Within 20 days after receiving a duly complete application for appointment of mediator, the Chief Judge of the Provincial People’s Court shall consider appointing a mediator; if the application is refused, the chief judge must provide explanation in writing.

5. Within 07 days after issuing the appointment decision, the Chief Judge of the Provincial People’s Court shall publish the list of mediators on the website of the Provincial People’s Court and post up at the head office of the court where the mediator has worked; and at the same time send it to the Supreme People's Court for publication on the web portal of the Supreme People's Court.

6. The term of office of a mediator is 03 years from the date of appointment.

7. The Chief Justice of the Supreme People's Court shall elaborate this Article.

Article 12. Re-appointment of mediators

1. A mediator, upon the end of his/her term of office is considered for reappointment, except for the following cases:
a) He/she is no longer fit to work;

b) He has not completed the task;

c) He/she is among 10% of the total number of mediators of the court for which he/she works who in the past 02 years have the poorest level of performance and need to be substituted.

2. An application for re-appointment of a mediator comprises:

a) An application form for re-appointment;
b) A fitness to work certificate issued by a competent health authority;

