3. Persons assigned by medical examination and treatment establishments or other organizations competent to provide psychological treatment or counseling to conduct psychological treatment or counseling at the place of residence of the juvenile delinquent shall cooperate with the direct supervisor and the juvenile’s family in managing and providing psychological treatment or counseling.
4. Medical examination and treatment establishments and other organizations competent to provide psychological treatment or counseling, as selected by the presiding authorities, shall have the following responsibilities:

a) Receive, monitor, conduct medical examination and treatment, or provide psychological treatment or counseling to juvenile delinquents participating in psychological treatment or counseling;

b) Develop intervention plans and prepare dossiers for juvenile delinquents participating in psychological treatment or counseling;

c) Periodically assess the results of medical treatment or psychological treatment or counseling on a monthly basis, an ad hoc basis, or when participants violate relevant obligations, and send such assessments to the direct supervisor for reporting to the President of the commune-level People’s Committee.

Chapter III

RESPONSIBILITIES AND PROCEDURES FOR COMMUNITY-BASED DIVERSION

Article 5. Agencies, organizations, and individuals responsible for execution of decisions on application of

community-based diversion measures
1. The commune-level People’s Committee, where persons serving community-based diversion measures reside, shall have the following tasks and powers:

a) Perform the tasks and powers prescribed in Clause 1 Article 73 of the Law on Juvenile Justice;

b) Develop plans for the implementation of community-based diversion measures and assign specific tasks to agencies, organizations, and individuals in the execution of decisions on the application of diversion measures;

